/*
    @std/time/time.qz - 04/08/2026
    Time and duration operations for Quarzum (Linux)

    This is the entry point of the time library. It groups together:

        - Duration: a length of time, stored as nanoseconds.
        - DateTime: an absolute point in time (Unix epoch).
        - Timer:   measures elapsed time using the monotonic clock.

    A Duration is created with the factory functions ofNanos,
    ofMicros, ofMillis, ofSeconds, ofMinutes and ofHours, and can
    be queried in any unit or formatted with toString():

        const d = ofSeconds(90);
        println(d.getMinutes());   // 1
        println(d.toString());     // 1m 30s

    A DateTime is captured with the now() function and exposes the
    calendar components through getYear(), getMonth(), getDay(),
    getHour(), getMinute() and getSecond():

        const now = now();
        println(now.toString());   // 2026-08-04 12:34:56

    A Timer records its starting point when created, and elapsed()
    returns the time spent since then as a Duration:

        const t = new Timer();
        ofMillis(100).sleep();
        println(t.elapsed().getMillis());

    Example:
        const start = now();
        println(start.toString());
        ofSeconds(1).sleep();
        const elapsed = now().sub(start);
        println(elapsed.toString());
*/
import "@std/time/duration.qz"
import "@std/time/datetime.qz"
import "@std/time/timer.qz"
